e legal limit. If those amounts were spread as in the band immediately below, 26.0 contracts would be expected. The observed-to-expected ratio is 5.5, with a 95% confidence interval of 4.3 to 7.0. December accounts for 27% of the contracts just under the limit, compared with 15% of all its services and supplies contracts. The awardee with the most contracts just under the limit, called awardee A here, has 19. Contract [1], for 14,955.38 euros, was awarded in December 2025.\nWhat it cannot show: The concentration of contracts in December does not indicate improper activity. Similar patterns can occur due to seasonal demand or budget cycles. Small amounts near the limit do not prove violations, as market prices or budget constraints may influence decisions.\nWhat can be checked: Check the dates and values of December contracts to see if they align with seasonal needs. Review awardee A's contracts to determine if multiple contracts serve the same purpose. Look for repeated orders from the same provider to assess if they reflect ongoing requirements or potential issues.","origen_es":"modelo","origen_en":"modelo","modelo":"Qwen3-4B (Q4_K_M, llama.cpp)","regla":"ss","citas":["I77Iq%2BXE9uN6nTs9LZ9RhQ%3D%3D","saBs1OR%2F9td%2FP7lJ7Fu0SA%3D%3D","%2FKmms4XML%2BBPpzdqOdhuWg%3D%3D"],"generado":"2026-11-15"}}